 {:db/id "q-15178/ma-source-field/f6c5e34ff5828865e27ec530ec76d4d619630618b822d64fc0960d7d08bfddc8" :answer-field/id #uuid "9286f2bc-6874-57c4-a822-f4b4566f80e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-15178/ma-source-field/f6c5e34ff5828865e27ec530ec76d4d619630618b822d64fc0960d7d08bfddc8/5ce5738ebf6d1d9f9a94e838ac9b535b878d4e1a8c2b5c97281814e84c17c502" "q-15178/ma-source-field/f6c5e34ff5828865e27ec530ec76d4d619630618b822d64fc0960d7d08bfddc8/92754e33e0ca3179f8830ccc9d31117e683483f0d0561f2ccc08d3669e74d889" "q-15178/ma-source-field/f6c5e34ff5828865e27ec530ec76d4d619630618b822d64fc0960d7d08bfddc8/372d1516d28a34fec153af7e3f79c55a812b0e3eb0e76b19bcd6c9b14b09c206" "q-15178/ma-source-field/f6c5e34ff5828865e27ec530ec76d4d619630618b822d64fc0960d7d08bfddc8/6d64004f0f279535da737fd2b6d0a28296f3aed4bda0818d662cfb60721153b2" "q-15178/ma-source-field/f6c5e34ff5828865e27ec530ec76d4d619630618b822d64fc0960d7d08bfddc8/6732318ae31777557413875c2bfab1df5911db6b432215cc29f736de6d1e75b2"] :answer-field/correct "q-15178/ma-source-field/f6c5e34ff5828865e27ec530ec76d4d619630618b822d64fc0960d7d08bfddc8/6732318ae31777557413875c2bfab1df5911db6b432215cc29f736de6d1e75b2" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-15178" :question/id #uuid "9a2f2890-829e-5660-aa0e-71dea52c1f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-15178" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca290e8fd51edddf4381ec267b8e0a5692d0e52bd2a36b7d43ab89cbeb5cf00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-15178/ma-source-field/f6c5e34ff5828865e27ec530ec76d4d619630618b822d64fc0960d7d08bfddc8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
