 {:db/id "q-15692" :question/id #uuid "b39c00d5-06a8-557a-8fa0-abcc975f62c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15692" :question/problem "![](images/18/185393f07b920bf9b72799f1c17c4245017b500ff1ca92a0104fe95ce621bde4.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=9,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (0-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ 4+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =9-4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15692/ma-source-field/dc743ca33018d9dfda1717e86f69780b3af5a29cf97a442bf5ee963362f2a7ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
