 {:db/id "q-156978" :question/id #uuid "428cd283-358d-5b3a-86aa-024fc5c3885c" :question/math-academy-id "q-156978" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={sin}^{2}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Which of the following could be $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={sin}^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-156978/ma-source-field/5ed0a2f559d4bf9f92e3efa84e15ff61e717ff34b261ccd51343464deb7699ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
