 {:db/id "q-157052/ma-source-field/f9736db5f11327da453f6850a4e5f8db784904545c87be94c26cb15e292004ef" :answer-field/id #uuid "474be29c-51a5-5850-8515-5dee1fd67caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-157052/ma-source-field/f9736db5f11327da453f6850a4e5f8db784904545c87be94c26cb15e292004ef/b9bd15a01fd24d4ba8ad95507d3a29383dd0d145b707853eb95c85593d117d44" "q-157052/ma-source-field/f9736db5f11327da453f6850a4e5f8db784904545c87be94c26cb15e292004ef/8d9516eec434b0ae312a1a256b94851b8f42f29d7c2717da2ca6b2734350b991" "q-157052/ma-source-field/f9736db5f11327da453f6850a4e5f8db784904545c87be94c26cb15e292004ef/e4007722f40c27372da2342f39b603004233385ff8442302ec127a8f66a96a8a" "q-157052/ma-source-field/f9736db5f11327da453f6850a4e5f8db784904545c87be94c26cb15e292004ef/2ff8636675e91b6613b618617a35b92b67080e4e10f7bcdbc74561b36990dcee" "q-157052/ma-source-field/f9736db5f11327da453f6850a4e5f8db784904545c87be94c26cb15e292004ef/bb8eb8da4f10defd621de07355f28ff63c96b766e7ff60e35e5c6a042175efc5"] :answer-field/correct "q-157052/ma-source-field/f9736db5f11327da453f6850a4e5f8db784904545c87be94c26cb15e292004ef/bb8eb8da4f10defd621de07355f28ff63c96b766e7ff60e35e5c6a042175efc5" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-157052" :question/id #uuid "f81ecce3-b49e-5261-aee2-21f0b16a1f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-157052" :question/problem "$$\n\\boxed{}\\,x=\\boxed{}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and ${Q}^{T}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-157052/ma-source-field/f9736db5f11327da453f6850a4e5f8db784904545c87be94c26cb15e292004ef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
