 {:db/id "q-160608/ma-source-field/3d5837c849c8fcc69646d02dd6b4272e5ed10a2a7232f454ebbdde82213d6279" :answer-field/id #uuid "1398b2db-d522-591e-9af9-65f757fcf9ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-160608/ma-source-field/3d5837c849c8fcc69646d02dd6b4272e5ed10a2a7232f454ebbdde82213d6279/49fe06a93209a00c1bf544153f48b816c06cd02157f9c4fa170171220faf2ac9" "q-160608/ma-source-field/3d5837c849c8fcc69646d02dd6b4272e5ed10a2a7232f454ebbdde82213d6279/5501470bad59521bbf82667e9eaf3993172b51e31fecdd8c7bb376a61ef78362" "q-160608/ma-source-field/3d5837c849c8fcc69646d02dd6b4272e5ed10a2a7232f454ebbdde82213d6279/4b74349193df0b5a137e5a588337dc0be99fe94f8e5a414fb93ce26c70b3ba7f" "q-160608/ma-source-field/3d5837c849c8fcc69646d02dd6b4272e5ed10a2a7232f454ebbdde82213d6279/751be333201fdcd71e7e10ca01f102e62bb0d18cb955aefb605a3e0b6f44b1ed" "q-160608/ma-source-field/3d5837c849c8fcc69646d02dd6b4272e5ed10a2a7232f454ebbdde82213d6279/8942f425d3e3f2b1451e55dce44138589d19ad879d7b274ba8b61f42619d7a26"] :answer-field/correct "q-160608/ma-source-field/3d5837c849c8fcc69646d02dd6b4272e5ed10a2a7232f454ebbdde82213d6279/5501470bad59521bbf82667e9eaf3993172b51e31fecdd8c7bb376a61ef78362" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-160608" :question/id #uuid "5f849a8c-d5a9-5291-8dbf-bee7c6e87ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160608" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-{u}^{2},\\,y=u+v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-{u}^{2},\\,u+v) \\\\  & =(v-{1}^{2},\\,1+v) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,v+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=v+1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/f3/f304c409cfc62de3d296690c0acd2cec5b9726c03d8a196ed69de36d8ebbefd2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-160608/ma-source-field/3d5837c849c8fcc69646d02dd6b4272e5ed10a2a7232f454ebbdde82213d6279"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
