 {:db/id "q-161072/ma-source-field/142353a52eedf622e14b5b78b4050f28a3bfd341ba657966f6eb494931b48373" :answer-field/id #uuid "09e4fe92-85aa-505b-a8c5-4df7656265ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-161072/ma-source-field/142353a52eedf622e14b5b78b4050f28a3bfd341ba657966f6eb494931b48373/9d74eb82d04cda9ff4b17bb80e6fc1cc1c09fb0b3a30039f476e3f00d3ad20f7" "q-161072/ma-source-field/142353a52eedf622e14b5b78b4050f28a3bfd341ba657966f6eb494931b48373/07efa0ff5885fb59a7cb0366c3e2a5cfad30a1520a95421db8c53a8b2e583777" "q-161072/ma-source-field/142353a52eedf622e14b5b78b4050f28a3bfd341ba657966f6eb494931b48373/a5cfc473b620ae5c123c09dcaa728fbaaee259b3e4e1b09c71a03b9f84e7aa11" "q-161072/ma-source-field/142353a52eedf622e14b5b78b4050f28a3bfd341ba657966f6eb494931b48373/86543def3248a83740ed6e39d830adafced4be0a198651b6514125bafcebea63" "q-161072/ma-source-field/142353a52eedf622e14b5b78b4050f28a3bfd341ba657966f6eb494931b48373/dd9fd34b513ca8947d42ee821564e1a9da5af743cc88844fa7bf07c452b1d07d"] :answer-field/correct "q-161072/ma-source-field/142353a52eedf622e14b5b78b4050f28a3bfd341ba657966f6eb494931b48373/9d74eb82d04cda9ff4b17bb80e6fc1cc1c09fb0b3a30039f476e3f00d3ad20f7" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-161072" :question/id #uuid "fd6b23cf-0f4d-571e-a46d-8551c4a4d4b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161072" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={x}^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{2},$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2{x}^{3},\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161072/ma-source-field/142353a52eedf622e14b5b78b4050f28a3bfd341ba657966f6eb494931b48373"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
